<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p><m>P(3^\circ\,15'\,\mathrm{N}, 38^\circ\,\mathrm{E})</m> and <m>Q(11^\circ\,40'\,\mathrm{N}, 38^\circ\,\mathrm{E})</m> are on the meridian <m>{38}^\circ\,\mathrm{E}</m>. Take <m>\pi = \tfrac{22}{7}</m> and <m>R = 6370\,\text{km}</m>. Distances in km within 0.1% of the exact value are accepted.</p><p>(a) Find the distance <m>PQ</m> along the meridian in nautical miles.</p><p><fillin characters="8" name="ans1"/> nm  </p><p>(b) Find <m>PQ</m> in kilometres, to the nearest km.</p><p><fillin characters="8" name="ans2"/> km  </p></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>(a) <m>P</m> and <m>Q</m> are on the same side of the equator, so subtract the latitudes. <m>{11}^\circ\,{40}' - {3}^\circ\,{15}' = {8}^\circ\,{25}'</m>. A meridian is half of a great circle, and a nautical mile is one minute of arc on a great circle, so the difference in minutes is the distance in nautical miles: <m>{8} \times 60 + {25} = {505}</m>, so <m>PQ = {505}</m> nm.</p><p>(b) The angle at the centre is <m>{505}' = {8.417}^\circ</m>, so</p><p><me>PQ = \frac{ {8.417} }{360} \times 2 \times \frac{22}{7} \times 6370 = {936.1} \approx {936}\,\text{km}.</me></p><p>Or use <m>1</m> nm <m>= 1.853\,\text{km}</m>: <m>{505} \times 1.853 = {935.8}\,\text{km}</m>.</p></p></solution>
    <answer><p><m>505</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>936.12037037</m></p></answer>
</stack-static>